-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Sub-pixel tetrahedra:** Standard triangular/quadrilateral primitives are not optimal for sub-pixel sized areas due to connectivity overhead. Point-based rendering solves this by using single points [S1].
-*   **Boundary Quality:** Boundary quality degrades because the single-point representation lacks scalar interpolation across the tetrahedron, limiting accurate pixel values when depth complexity is low [S1].
-*   **Malformed Tetrahedra:** Malformed tetrahedra cause Hardware-Assisted Visibility Sorting (HAVS) to produce erroneous renderings, but this point-based method remains robust and does not fail under these conditions [S1].
-*   **Wide Size Variation:** If tetrahedra sizes vary widely, directly rendering the mesh may be better than approximating projections, as point sizes for large tetrahedra increase more rapidly than for small ones, increasing fragment generation. However, culling in the fragment program can mitigate this, though testing and branching is expensive [S1].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Parallelized Accurate Sorting for Projected Tetrahedra
+Uses parallel sorting algorithms to handle the depth sorting requirement of PBVR and other projected methods more efficiently on the GPU. [Link](1128-parallelized-accurate-sorting-for-projected-tetrahedra.md)
+
+### Sorting-free Pre-integrated Projected Tetrahedra
+Eliminates the need for global sorting by using pre-integrated textures and specific footprint strategies to allow front-to-back compositing without explicit depth sorting. [Link](7491-sorting-free-pre-integrated-projected-tetrahedra.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The preprocessing step is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra. Per-frame, sorting is $O(N \log N)$ or $O(N)$ with efficient spatial sorting. GPU processing is $O(N)$ for vertex shading and fragment generation, with compositing being $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of fragments.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the point representations and transforms. No connectivity information is stored [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** The method achieves interactive framerates for large datasets (e.g., Heart, SPX, fighter, F16) [S1]. It is generally faster than current direct volume rendering approaches [S1].
-*   **Scaling:** Accuracy scales with approximation complexity; higher approximation (e.g., ellipsoidal vs. circular) yields better visual fidelity but increases fragment generation and decreases performance [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Preprocessing is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of tetrahedra. Per-frame sorting is $O(N \log N)$ on the CPU. Rendering is $O(F)$ where $F$ is the number of fragments generated, which depends on the footprint shape and LOD.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the point representations and transformation matrices.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Achieves interactive framerates on large datasets (e.g., Heart, SPX, fighter, F16) [S1].
